{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"results\": [{\"id\": \"problem.md\", \"total_lines\": 190, \"start\": 1, \"end\": 190, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 49, \"requested_end\": 220, \"has_more\": false, \"next_start\": null, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": null, \"text\": \"1: # Irreducibility after reduction of an admissible sl2 lattice\\n2: \\n3: ## Original exercise\\n4: \\n5: James E. Humphreys, *Introduction to Lie Algebras and Representation Theory*,\\n6: Graduate Texts in Mathematics 9, Springer, section 27, exercise 7,\\n7: printed page 164. The exercise-list convention on page 163 sets\\n8: \\\\(L=\\\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\\\) and identifies weights with integers.\\n9: Book DOI: [10.1007/978-1-4612-6398-2](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4612-6398-2).\\n10: \\n11: > 7. If \\\\(0\\\\leq\\\\lambda<\\\\operatorname{char}K\\\\), \\\\(V=V(\\\\lambda)\\\\),\\n12: > prove that \\\\(V(K)\\\\) is irreducible as \\\\(L(K)\\\\)-module.\\n13: \\n14: ## Definitions and complete target\\n15: \\n16: Let \\\\(p\\\\) be any prime, \\\\(K\\\\) any field of characteristic \\\\(p\\\\), and\\n17: \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\) an integer with \\\\(0\\\\leq\\\\lambda<p\\\\).\\n18: Let \\\\(V(\\\\lambda)\\\\) be the complex irreducible highest-weight\\n19: \\\\(\\\\mathfrak{sl}_2(\\\\mathbb C)\\\\)-module of highest weight \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\),\\n20: of dimension \\\\(\\\\lambda+1\\\\). Its Chevalley generators satisfy\\n21: \\n22: \\\\[\\n23: [h,x]=2x,\\\\qquad[h,y]=-2y,\\\\qquad[x,y]=h.\\n24: \\\\]\\n25: \\n26: Choose a nonzero highest-weight vector \\\\(v\\\\) with \\\\(xv=0\\\\) and\\n27: \\\\(hv=\\\\lambda v\\\\). Let \\\\(U_{\\\\mathbb Z}\\\\) be the integer subalgebra of\\n28: the universal enveloping algebra generated by all divided powers\\n29: \\\\(X_r=x^r/r!\\\\), \\\\(Y_r=y^r/r!\\\\), for integers \\\\(r\\\\geq0\\\\).\\n30: An **admissible lattice** \\\\(M\\\\subset V(\\\\lambda)\\\\) is a free abelian\\n31: subgroup with an integer basis that is also a complex basis, stable under\\n32: \\\\(U_{\\\\mathbb Z}\\\\). It is arbitrary, not necessarily the minimal lattice.\\n33: \\n34: Put \\\\(V(K)=M\\\\otimes_{\\\\mathbb Z}K\\\\). The Lie algebra\\n35: \\\\(L(K)=(\\\\mathbb Zx+\\\\mathbb Zy+\\\\mathbb Zh)\\\\otimes_{\\\\mathbb Z}K\\\\)\\n36: acts by the induced lattice operators. This is the Chevalley\\n37: \\\\(\\\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\\\) construction also in characteristic 2.\\n38: Prove that \\\\(V(K)\\\\) is nonzero and every \\\\(K\\\\)-linear subspace\\n39: invariant under \\\\(x,y,h\\\\) is zero or all of \\\\(V(K)\\\\).\\n40: Both \\\\(\\\\lambda=0\\\\) and \\\\(p=2\\\\) are included; \\\\(K\\\\) need not be\\n41: algebraically closed.\\n42: \\n43: ## Fixed natural-language proof\\n44: \\n45: ### 1. Divided-power coordinates in characteristic zero\\n46: \\n47: Put \\\\(n=\\\\lambda+1\\\\) and \\\\(e_i=y^iv/i!\\\\) for \\\\(0\\\\leq i\\\\leq\\\\lambda\\\\).\\n48: The commutator identities give, by induction,\\n49: \\n50: \\\\[\\n51: hy^iv=(\\\\lambda-2i)y^iv,\\\\qquad\\n52: xy^iv=i(\\\\lambda-i+1)y^{i-1}v\\\\quad(i\\\\geq1).\\n53: \\\\]\\n54: \\n55: For \\\\(i\\\\leq\\\\lambda\\\\), applying \\\\(x^i\\\\) to \\\\(y^iv\\\\) gives\\n56: \\\\(i!\\\\lambda(\\\\lambda-1)\\\\cdots(\\\\lambda-i+1)v\\\\ne0\\\\) over \\\\(\\\\mathbb C\\\\).\\n57: At \\\\(i=0\\\\) the empty product is one. Thus every \\\\(e_i\\\\ne0\\\\).\\n58: Their \\\\(h\\\\)-eigenvalues \\\\(\\\\lambda-2i\\\\) are distinct over \\\\(\\\\mathbb C\\\\),\\n59: so they are linearly independent: applying\\n60: \\\\(\\\\prod_{k\\\\ne j}(h-(\\\\lambda-2k)\\\\mathrm{Id})\\\\) to a relation isolates\\n61: its \\\\(j\\\\)-th coefficient with a nonzero multiplier.\\n62: There are \\\\(n\\\\) vectors, the dimension of \\\\(V(\\\\lambda)\\\\), hence they\\n63: form a basis. Also \\\\(y^{\\\\lambda+1}v=0\\\\), since otherwise it would be\\n64: an eigenvector of eigenvalue \\\\(-\\\\lambda-2\\\\), absent from this basis.\\n65: All higher lowering powers vanish as well.\\n66: \\n67: The operator matrices are therefore\\n68: \\n69: \\\\[\\n70: xe_0=0,\\\\quad xe_i=(\\\\lambda-i+1)e_{i-1}\\\\ (i\\\\geq1),\\\\qquad\\n71: ye_i=(i+1)e_{i+1}\\\\ (i<\\\\lambda),\\\\quad ye_\\\\lambda=0,\\n72: \\\\quad he_i=(\\\\lambda-2i)e_i.\\n73: \\\\]\\n74: \\n75: Iteration gives the integral divided-power matrices\\n76: \\n77: \\\\[\\n78: X_re_i=\\\\binom{\\\\lambda-i+r}{r}e_{i-r}\\\\quad(0\\\\leq r\\\\leq i),\\n79: \\\\qquad\\n80: Y_re_i=\\\\binom{i+r}{r}e_{i+r}\\\\quad(i+r\\\\leq\\\\lambda).       \\\\tag{1}\\n81: \\\\]\\n82: \\n83: The respective expressions are zero when \\\\(r>i\\\\) or\\n84: \\\\(i+r>\\\\lambda\\\\), by termination of the strings just proved.\\n85: \\n86: ### 2. Matrix units preserve the localized arbitrary lattice\\n87: \\n88: Let \\\\(R=\\\\mathbb Z_{(p)}=\\\\{a/b\\\\in\\\\mathbb Q:p\\\\nmid b\\\\}\\\\), and regard\\n89: \\\\(M_R=R\\\\otimes_{\\\\mathbb Z}M\\\\) as a submodule of \\\\(V(\\\\lambda)\\\\).\\n90: The embedding is injective because an integer basis of \\\\(M\\\\) is\\n91: complex-linearly independent. The module \\\\(M_R\\\\) is free of rank \\\\(n\\\\)\\n92: over \\\\(R\\\\) and is preserved by all \\\\(X_r,Y_r\\\\), by admissibility.\\n93: \\n94: Define \\\\(E_{ij}\\\\) to send \\\\(e_j\\\\) to \\\\(e_i\\\\) and all other basis\\n95: vectors to zero. Formula (1) gives\\n96: \\n97: \\\\[\\n98: P:=Y_\\\\lambda X_\\\\lambda=E_{\\\\lambda\\\\lambda},\\\\qquad\\n99: X_{\\\\lambda-i}P=E_{i\\\\lambda},\\\\qquad\\n100: P Y_{\\\\lambda-j}=\\\\binom\\\\lambda j E_{\\\\lambda j}.\\n101: \\\\]\\n102: \\n103: For the last identity, an input index below \\\\(j\\\\) is sent below\\n104: \\\\(e_\\\\lambda\\\\) and killed by \\\\(P\\\\); an index above \\\\(j\\\\) is sent past\\n105: the end of the lowering string. On \\\\(e_j\\\\) the coefficient is precisely\\n106: \\\\(\\\\binom\\\\lambda j\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\lambda<p\\\\), none of\\n107: \\\\(\\\\lambda!\\\\), \\\\(j!\\\\), \\\\((\\\\lambda-j)!\\\\) is divisible by \\\\(p\\\\), so\\n108: \\\\(\\\\binom\\\\lambda j\\\\) is a unit in \\\\(R\\\\). Consequently\\n109: \\n110: \\\\[\\n111: E_{ij}=\\\\left(\\\\binom\\\\lambda j\\\\right)^{-1}\\n112:        X_{\\\\lambda-i}P Y_{\\\\lambda-j}.                 \\\\tag{2}\\n113: \\\\]\\n114: \\n115: Every matrix unit preserves \\\\(M_R\\\\). No assertion about distinct\\n116: weights modulo \\\\(p\\\\) has been used.\\n117: \\n118: ### 3. Recover a standard basis for the arbitrary lattice\\n119: \\n120: Define \\\\(J_i=\\\\{c\\\\in\\\\mathbb C:c e_i\\\\in M_R\\\\}\\\\).\\n121: The coordinate projection \\\\(E_{ii}\\\\) maps \\\\(M_R\\\\) onto \\\\(J_i e_i\\\\).\\n122: Applying \\\\(E_{ij}\\\\) to \\\\(c e_j\\\\) shows \\\\(J_j\\\\subseteq J_i\\\\);\\n123: reversing the indices gives equality. Let \\\\(J\\\\) be their common module.\\n124: It is finitely generated, being a projection of a finite free module,\\n125: and nonzero because \\\\(M_R\\\\) spans \\\\(V(\\\\lambda)\\\\) over \\\\(\\\\mathbb C\\\\).\\n126: The coordinate projections and independence of the \\\\(e_i\\\\) give\\n127: \\n128: \\\\[\\n129: M_R=\\\\bigoplus_{i=0}^{\\\\lambda}J e_i.                  \\\\tag{3}\\n130: \\\\]\\n131: \\n132: One must still prove that \\\\(J\\\\) has rank one; containment in\\n133: \\\\(\\\\mathbb C\\\\) alone does not imply that. Let\\n134: \\\\(r=\\\\dim_{\\\\mathbb Q}(\\\\mathbb Q\\\\otimes_R J)\\\\), which is finite.\\n135: Tensoring (3) with the fraction field \\\\(\\\\mathbb Q\\\\) and using the\\n136: rank \\\\(n\\\\) of \\\\(M_R\\\\) gives \\\\(n=nr\\\\), so \\\\(r=1\\\\), since \\\\(n>0\\\\).\\n137: The multiplication map \\\\(\\\\mathbb Q\\\\otimes_R J\\\\to\\\\mathbb C\\\\) is\\n138: injective: this tensor product is the localization of the torsion-free\\n139: module \\\\(J\\\\subset\\\\mathbb C\\\\), and a fraction mapping to zero has\\n140: zero numerator. Thus \\\\(J\\\\) spans a one-dimensional rational line.\\n141: \\n142: Choose \\\\(0\\\\ne a\\\\in J\\\\) and write a finite set of nonzero generators\\n143: as \\\\(q_1a,\\\\ldots,q_ka\\\\) with \\\\(q_j\\\\in\\\\mathbb Q^\\\\times\\\\).\\n144: Each \\\\(q_j=p^{\\\\nu_j}u_j\\\\) for \\\\(\\\\nu_j\\\\in\\\\mathbb Z\\\\) and a unit\\n145: \\\\(u_j\\\\) of \\\\(R\\\\). Let \\\\(\\\\nu=\\\\min_j\\\\nu_j\\\\). All generators belong\\n146: to \\\\(p^\\\\nu R a\\\\), and a generator with minimal exponent generates\\n147: that whole module. Therefore \\\\(J=Rb\\\\) for a nonzero \\\\(b\\\\in J\\\\).\\n148: By (3), \\\\(b e_0,\\\\ldots,b e_\\\\lambda\\\\) is an \\\\(R\\\\)-basis of \\\\(M_R\\\\).\\n149: \\n150: ### 4. Reduce and extend scalars\\n151: \\n152: All denominators in \\\\(R\\\\) are invertible in \\\\(K\\\\), so the integer map\\n153: extends to \\\\(R\\\\to K\\\\). The natural tensor maps give\\n154: \\n155: \\\\[\\n156: M\\\\otimes_{\\\\mathbb Z}K\\\\cong M_R\\\\otimes_R K\\n157: \\\\cong(M_R/pM_R)\\\\otimes_{\\\\mathbb F_p}K.\\n158: \\\\]\\n159: \\n160: For example the first map sends \\\\(m\\\\otimes c\\\\) to\\n161: \\\\((1\\\\otimes m)\\\\otimes c\\\\); its inverse sends\\n162: \\\\((r\\\\otimes m)\\\\otimes c\\\\) to \\\\(m\\\\otimes\\\\bar r c\\\\).\\n163: The reduced images \\\\(b_0,\\\\ldots,b_\\\\lambda\\\\) of \\\\(b e_i\\\\) form a\\n164: \\\\(K\\\\)-basis, with exactly the matrices of step 1 reduced into \\\\(K\\\\).\\n165: The matrix-unit identities (2) persist on this basis.\\n166: \\n167: For \\\\(0\\\\leq r\\\\leq\\\\lambda<p\\\\), \\\\(r!\\\\ne0\\\\) in \\\\(K\\\\), and the induced\\n168: \\\\(X_r,Y_r\\\\) equal \\\\((r!)^{-1}x^r,(r!)^{-1}y^r\\\\). Hence any\\n169: \\\\(K\\\\)-subspace invariant under \\\\(x,y\\\\) is invariant under every matrix\\n170: unit in (2).\\n171: \\n172: ### 5. Conclude irreducibility\\n173: \\n174: Let \\\\(W\\\\) be invariant. If it is nonzero, choose\\n175: \\\\(0\\\\ne w=\\\\sum_i c_i b_i\\\\in W\\\\), and an index \\\\(j\\\\) with \\\\(c_j\\\\ne0\\\\).\\n176: For every \\\\(i\\\\), \\\\(E_{ij}w=c_jb_i\\\\in W\\\\). Division by \\\\(c_j\\\\)\\n177: gives \\\\(b_i\\\\in W\\\\), so all basis vectors lie in \\\\(W\\\\) and\\n178: \\\\(W=V(K)\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\dim_K V(K)=\\\\lambda+1>0\\\\), it is nonzero and\\n179: irreducible. If \\\\(\\\\lambda=0\\\\), all relevant factorials and binomial\\n180: coefficients are one and the argument applies unchanged. For \\\\(p=2\\\\),\\n181: the allowed \\\\(\\\\lambda\\\\) are zero and one; the proof still works\\n182: without requiring distinct reduced \\\\(h\\\\)-eigenvalues.\\n183: \\n184: ## Input status\\n185: \\n186: This file fixes the exercise, notation and supplied proof, including the\\n187: arbitrary-lattice argument. It is not an NL0 artifact, an Audit A receipt,\\n188: a Lean proof, or a passing test result. Future runs must construct and check\\n189: those artifacts from these explicit inputs, registering any foundational\\n190: module or localization results used by their NL0.\"}, {\"id\": \"signature-evidence.json\", \"pointer\": \"\", \"view\": \"value\", \"value\": {\"M001\": [{\"probe_version\": 1, \"started_at\": \"2026-10-24T09:36:08.229555+00:00\", \"finished_at\": \"2026-10-24T09:36:10.971985+00:00\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"lean_version\": \"Lean (version 4.32.2, arm64-apple-darwin24.6.0, commit f3b06c705e6c85f5314019d5d3baab0fec5b580c, Release)\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"execution_boundary\": \"offline native Lean sandbox\", \"sources\": {\"M001\": {\"declaration\": \"Module.finrank_pi_fintype\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Dimension.Constructions\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"signature\": \"@Module.finrank_pi_fintype : ∀ (R : Type u) [inst : Semiring.{u} R] [StrongRankCondition.{u} R] {ι : Type v} [inst_2 : Fintype.{v} ι]\\n  {M : ι → Type w} [inst_3 : (i : ι) → AddCommMonoid.{w} (M i)] [inst_4 : (i : ι) → _root_.Module.{u, w} R (M i)]\\n  [∀ (i : ι), Module.Free.{u, w} R (M i)] [∀ (i : ι), Module.Finite.{u, w} R (M i)],\\n  Eq.{1} (Module.finrank.{u, max v w} R ((i : ι) → M i)) (∑ i, Module.finrank.{u, w} R